    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to understand the science behind my sourdough starter a bit better. specifically how do the lactobacillus and the yeast (saccharomyces cerevisiae) actually interact during the fermentation process? like how do they create the acidity and the bubbles? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During sourdough fermentation, lactobacillus bacteria and Saccharomyces cerevisiae yeast work together symbiotically. The lactobacillus bacteria produce lactic acid, which creates the sour taste and maintains a low pH environment that the yeast can thrive in. Meanwhile, the yeast ferments the carbohydrates Present with Saccharomyces cerevisiae ferments the sugars in the flour, producing carbon dioxide gas, which causes the leavening (bubbles) in the dough. The bacteria also produce alcohol and organic acids, further contributing to the flavor and texture of the sourdough. Essentially, lactobacillus helps keep the environment favorable for yeast, while yeast generates the carbon dioxide needed for leavening and the alcohol that complements the sour flavor brought by the bacteria.",
