    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i was reading somewhere that the bacteria actually help protect the yeast from other types of bad bacteria or mold by making it too acidic for them to survive. is that how the starter stays \"stable\" over time even when it's just sitting there? like how do they compete with the \"bad\" microbes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, that's correct! The lactic acid bacteria (LAB) in sourdough starters produce organic acids, notably lactic and acetic acid, which lowers the pH of the mix, creating an acidic (and thus hostile) environment for many undesirable microorganisms. This acidity acts as a preservation method, preventing the overgrowth of pathogenic bacteria, spoilage organisms, and mold, which cannot survive well in low pH environments. In addition, LAB produce bacteriocins that can inhibit the growth of non-sourdough bacteria and some molds. Moreover, the production of carbon dioxide by the yeast creates pockets of fermentation that are less favorable for microbial growth. This competitive exclusion helps to keep the sourdough starter \"stable,\" allowing the beneficial Lactobacillus and Saccharomyces cerevisiae to maintain their populations over time, even when not fed regularly. This dynamic equilibrium of bacteria and yeast alongside these acidifying processes helps to ensure the microbial health and longevity of the sourdough starter.",
      "source": "target",
